codex
Use of Switch: 7/10 — Linked rooms, team-scoped agents, and templates could make Switch central, but the key permissions and data flow are unproven.

Technical execution: 2/10 — Nothing is built or run, so cloning and reproducing the workflow cannot yet be assessed.

Creativity and impact: 7/10 — Cross-team migration gated on owner sign-off and evidence is a useful, specific workflow; its impact is not yet measured.

Presentation and documentation: 3/10 — The plan is detailed, but there is no working README or demo video.

Weighted total: 4.95/10 (0.30×7 + 0.25×2 + 0.25×7 + 0.20×3).

Shortlist: No. The concept is promising, but the submission is still a plan with no runnable evidence.

Three biggest weaknesses, most costly first:

1. Nothing is built. Implement the end-to-end demo path, publish setup instructions, and show a clean clone-and-run reproduction.
2. Switch’s role and privacy guarantees are asserted, not demonstrated. Show that team agents can access only their own repos and that only the fixed impact slot crosses room boundaries.
3. The impact claim is unverified. Run the historical replay on named public repos and report missed consumers, false positives, and limitations.

Claim I doubt: “only the slot crosses” between private rooms. Linked rooms and addressing policies may support coordination, but the plan does not show that they enforce field-level isolation or prevent other conversation content from being exposed.
